        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best snow shovel to keep in a car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most drivers, a compact aluminum shovel that breaks down or telescopes, like the Lifeline Aluminum Sport Utility Shovel, is the best balance. It fits under a cargo floor, extends to a usable length and digs into hard plow spoil. Drivers who often face deep snow far from help should consider an avalanche-grade shovel like the BCA Dozer 1T.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a plastic snow shovel good enough for a car?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only in light-snow regions. Plastic blades work on powder and are safe on paint, but they flex and skate off the compacted, partly frozen ridge a plow leaves along parked cars. If you park on plowed streets or drive rural roads, choose an aluminum or steel blade instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an avalanche shovel as a car shovel?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the best options. Avalanche shovels are built to move dense, chunky debris, which is very similar to plow spoil. The catch is cost and that skiers often keep theirs in a backpack, so make sure one stays in the car rather than relying on a shovel that is somewhere else.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big should a car snow shovel be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A blade around 9 to 10 inches wide is a good balance between moving snow and fitting in a wheel well. For storage, measure the space first: folding spades fit under a seat at around 9 to 10 inches, three-piece shovels lie flat beside a spare, and telescoping avalanche shovels need about 22 to 24 inches of trunk space.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to clear the tailpipe if my car is stuck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you are running the engine. News reports relaying NHTSA winter driving guidance say to keep the exhaust clear of snow and ice to avoid carbon monoxide poisoning, run the engine only in short periods and crack a window. Carbon monoxide has no smell, so check the tailpipe every time you restart.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What else should I keep in my car in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Alongside a shovel, carry an ice scraper and brush, sand or cat litter or traction mats, jumper cables, a blanket, warm gloves and a hat, a phone charger, water and some food. A bright cloth to tie to the antenna helps rescuers find you. Keep it all in one bag so nothing gets left behind.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a folding entrenching tool good for snow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is excellent for chopping ice and hard plow ridges and fits under a seat, but the small blade moves loose snow slowly. A tool like the Gerber E-Tool is a great choice for a small car or as an ice-breaker alongside a larger shovel, but it is not the fastest way to clear deep snow.
              </p>
            </div>

